+\section{Method}
+\label{sec:method}
+
+\subsection{Text-attributed graphs}
+\todo{Formalize: corpus as directed graph $G=(V,E)$, node = document with a token
+sequence, edge $(A,B)$ = a detected link from $A$ to $B$. Link detectors run online.}
+
+\subsection{Graph-traversal packing}
+\todo{BFS/DFS/random-walk/random; token budget + per-doc token cap; why order matters
+for causal cross-doc attention (a doc can only attend to earlier docs in the pack).}
+
+\subsection{Link-aware attention masks}
+The four mask conditions, ordered by attention permitted
+($\doccausal < \crossdoc \le \concatlink \le \concat$):
+\begin{description}
+  \item[\doccausal] block-diagonal causal; documents fully isolated (baseline).
+  \item[\crossdoc] when $A$ links to $B$ and both are packed, tokens in $A$ from the
+        link position onward attend to all of $B$; asymmetric, causal order kept.
+  \item[\concatlink] compute control: grant from the \emph{entire} source document,
+        not just the link position (same connectivity, more attention).
+  \item[\concat] compute control: connected components merged into one causal
+        super-document (most attention, no inference-time linking).
+\end{description}
+\todo{Figure: the four masks on one 16k batch (reuse docs/images/mask_*.png).}
+\todo{Formal mask definition (indicator over token pairs); note FlexAttention block
+mask + custom Triton kernels make 32k-context sparse training tractable.}
